### Reasoning for IEC 62443 and SCADA Integration
To align with IEC 62443 standards, the control system must segment the SCADA communication into a distinct zone with a well-defined conduit to the controller. Data originating from SCADA (like remote setpoints) must be sanitized and bounded to prevent malicious or accidental out-of-range inputs from jeopardizing the physical safety of the fume hood operators. Dual-redundant failover guarantees availability, ensuring that if the primary exhaust fan fails, the secondary fan immediately takes over without dropping the face velocity below lethal thresholds.

### Siemens SCL Code
```pascal
FUNCTION_BLOCK "FB_FumeHood_Reasoning"
VAR_INPUT
    SCADA_Enable : Bool;
    SCADA_Velocity_SP : Real;
    FaceVelocity_PV : Real;
    FanA_Fault : Bool;
    FanB_Fault : Bool;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    FanA_Command : Real;
    FanB_Command : Real;
    ActiveFan : Int; // 1 = Fan A, 2 = Fan B
    SecurityZone_Alarm : Bool;
END_VAR
VAR
    PID_Controller : "PID_Compact";
    Validated_SP : Real;
    Base_CV : Real;
END_VAR

BEGIN
    // IEC 62443 Conduit Sanitation (Limit setpoints to safe boundaries)
    IF #SCADA_Velocity_SP < 0.3 OR #SCADA_Velocity_SP > 1.0 THEN
        #SecurityZone_Alarm := TRUE;
        #Validated_SP := 0.5; // Fallback safe setpoint
    ELSE
        #SecurityZone_Alarm := FALSE;
        #Validated_SP := #SCADA_Velocity_SP;
    END_IF;

    // VAV PID
    #PID_Controller(
        Setpoint := #Validated_SP,
        Input := #FaceVelocity_PV,
        Reset := NOT #SCADA_Enable,
        Output => #Base_CV
    );

    // Dual-Redundant Failover Logic
    IF NOT #FanA_Fault THEN
        #ActiveFan := 1;
        #FanA_Command := #Base_CV;
        #FanB_Command := 0.0;
    ELSIF NOT #FanB_Fault THEN
        #ActiveFan := 2;
        #FanB_Command := #Base_CV;
        #FanA_Command := 0.0;
    ELSE
        #ActiveFan := 0;
        #FanA_Command := 0.0;
        #FanB_Command := 0.0;
    END_IF;
END_FUNCTION_BLOCK
```

